238 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (The system architecture is built around three core design goals, applied at both the class and component level:)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
239 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 490 0 R
/E (High Cohesion : Achieved through domain-driven decomposition: each class \(e.g., WeddingHall, Reservation\) handles a single, focused responsibility, and the Application/Business Layer is split into nine functional components \(Hall Listing, Reservation Management, Payment Processing, etc.\). This keeps related logic together and improves maintainability and reusability.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
240 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 491 0 R
/E (Low Coupling : Classes and components communicate through well-defined interfaces \(methods, status flags\) rather than direct dependencies. Reservation, Payment, and Notification interact without knowing each other's internals, and all components route through shared infrastructure \(Persistence, Notification Gateway, Payment Gateway\). This enables flexibility, fault tolerance, and independent testing/deployment.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 492 0 R
/E (Security : Enforced via role-based access control across all layers, derived from non-functional requirements \(password rules, login attempt limits, restricted listing access, protected payment data\). Sensitive data is hidden at the class level \(private attributes, controlled methods\), and a centralized Security component \(Encryption + Access Control interfaces\) governs all sensitive operations system-wide.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
